==Venue==
[[File:EM-Stadion Wals-Siezenheim zur Euro.jpg|thumb|275px|The [[Red Bull Arena (Salzburg)|Red Bull Arena]] in [[Salzburg]] hosted the match.]]
It was the first UEFA Super Cup to be held in Austria, and the first major European final to be played in [[Salzburg]]. It was the seventh major European club final to be played in Austria, after four in the [[List of European Cup and UEFA Champions League finals|European Cup/Champions League]] ([[1964 European Cup final|1964]], [[1987 European Cup final|1987]], [[1990 European Cup final|1990]], [[1995 UEFA Champions League final|1995]]), one in the [[List of UEFA Cup Winners' Cup finals|Cup Winners' Cup]] ([[1970 European Cup Winners' Cup final|1970]]) and one in the [[List of UEFA Cup and Europa League finals|UEFA Cup]] ([[1994 UEFA Cup final#First leg|1994 first leg]]), all of which were held at the [[Ernst-Happel-Stadion]] in [[Vienna]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://apa.at/news/uefa-vergibt-supercup-2026-an-salzburg/ |title=UEFA vergibt Supercup 2026 an Salzburg |trans-title=UEFA awards Supercup to Salzburg 2026 |agency=[[Austria Press Agency]] |date=11 September 2025 |access-date=11 September 2025}}</ref> The [[Red Bull Arena (Salzburg)|Red Bull Arena]] is the home stadium of [[FC Red Bull Salzburg|Red Bull Salzburg]], and has a capacity of 30,071. The venue opened in 2003, and hosted three group stage matches at [[UEFA Euro 2008]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.redbullsalzburg.at/de/aktuelles/news/uefa-super-cup-2026-salzburg |title=UEFA Super Cup 2026 steigt in Salzburg |trans-title=UEFA Super Cup 2026 will take place in Salzburg |publisher=[[FC Red Bull Salzburg]] |date=11 September 2025 |access-date=11 September 2025}}</ref> It was known as Stadion Salzburg in UEFA communications due to their restrictions on third-party sponsorship.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/2019394--salzburg-vs-krasnodar/|title=UEFA Europa League - Salzburg-Krasnodar|access-date=10 January 2017|publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref>

===Host selection===
The Red Bull Arena was selected as the host by the UEFA Executive Committee during their meeting in [[Tirana]], Albania, on 11 September 2025.<ref name="ExCo decision">{{cite web |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/029d-1eb2cecb8955-8fa1e427770d-1000/ |title=Estadio Metropolitano to host 2027 UEFA Champions League final |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=11 September 2025 |access-date=11 September 2025}}</ref>

==Pre-match==
After being [[2026 FIFA World Cup immigration and visa controversies|denied entry]] to the United States to officiate at the [[2026 FIFA World Cup]], Somali referee [[Omar Artan]] was appointed as the lead official for the 2026 UEFA Super Cup on 11 June 2026, becoming the first ever non-European to officiate the match.<ref name="referee">{{cite news |title=Top African referee Omar Artan to officiate 2026 UEFA Super Cup after being unable to participate in FIFA World Cup 2026 |url=https://www.uefa.com/news-media/news/02a6-20d7ce07703f-bee983e79921-1000--top-african-referee-omar-artan-to-officiate-2026-uefa-super/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=11 June 2026 |accessdate=11 June 2026 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news
 |title=Artan to referee Uefa Super Cup after losing World Cup spot
 |work=Yahoo Sports
 |publisher=BBC Sport
 |date=11 June 2026
 |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/articles/artan-referee-uefa-super-cup-142504531.html
 |access-date=13 June 2026
 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260618100226/https://sports.yahoo.com/articles/artan-referee-uefa-super-cup-142504531.html
 |archive-date=18 June 2026
 |url-status=dead
}}</ref> The appointment was made within the framework of a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) between UEFA and the [[Confederation of African Football]] (CAF), signed on the sidelines of the 76th FIFA Congress in [[Vancouver]], Canada in April 2026.<ref name="referee" />

===Dignitaries in attendance===
Other officials who attended the match included UEFA President [[Aleksander Čeferin]] and General Secretary [[Theodore Theodoridis]], CAF President [[Patrice Motsepe]], [[Paris Saint-Germain FC]] President [[Nasser Al-Khelaifi]], [[Austrian Football Association]] (ÖFB) President [[Josef Pröll]] representing the host nation, and Rwandan President [[Paul Kagame]], whose country's [[Tourism in Rwanda|"Visit Rwanda" tourism campaign]] sponsors both teams.<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 August 2026 |title=Welcome to the UEFA Super Cup 2026! |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefasupercup/news/02a8-2150258c1bdc-d4d21724280c-1000--welcome-to-the-uefa-super-cup-2026/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=13 August 2026 |title=President Kagame attends PSG-Aston Villa Super Cup clash |url=https://en.igihe.com/sports/article/president-kagame-attends-psg-aston-villa-super-cup-clash |work=IGIHE News}}</ref> This match marked Čeferin’s first public appearance since his boycott of the [[2026 FIFA World Cup final]] match between [[Spain national football team|Spain]] and [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]], an action prompted by the [[2026 Trump–FIFA red card controversy|Balogun red card controversy]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Slater |first=Matt |date=20 July 2026 |title=UEFA president Aleksander Ceferin boycotts World Cup final in response to Folarin Balogun incident |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7458936/2026/07/20/ceferin-world-cup-final-infantino-fifa-uefa/ |work=[[The Athletic]] |publisher=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> <ref>{{Cite news |date=20 July 2026 |title=Ceferin skipped World Cup final reportedly to show disapproval of FIFA |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20260720-ceferin-skipped-world-cup-final-reportedly-to-show-disapproval-of-fifa |work=[[France 24]] |agency=[[Agence France-Presse]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Johnson |first=Dale |date=20 July 2026 |title=Uefa president boycotts World Cup final in protest against Fifa |url=http://bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cr59mg77ep2o |work=[[BBC Sport]]}}</ref>

==Match==
===Summary===
In the 20th minute, [[Khvicha Kvaratskhelia]] gave Paris Saint-Germain the lead when he received the ball on the left from [[Désiré Doué]] before cutting into the penalty area and shooting right footed to the left of the net. It was 1–1 in the 45th minute when [[Brian Madjo]] finished high to the net from six yards out with a side-footed volley after a cross from [[John McGinn]] from the right. This goal made him the youngest player ever to score at the UEFA Super Cup, at 17 years and 212 days.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/aug/12/uefa-super-cup-aston-villa-psg-match-report|title=Doué seals Super Cup glory for PSG after Madjo makes history for Aston Villa|date=12 August 2026|work=Guardian|accessdate=13 August 2026}}</ref>

In the 60th minute, Désiré Doué scored to make it 2–1 to Paris Saint-Germain when he received a pass on the right from substitute [[Ousmane Dembélé]] before cutting in on his left foot before shooting into the left of the net from the right edge of the six yard box. After a VAR check for offside, the goal was awarded.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cmn4n23z15elt|title=Paris Saint-Germain 2 Aston Villa 1|date=12 August 2026|work=BBC Sport|accessdate=13 August 2026}}</ref>

Following this result, Paris Saint-Germain became the fourth club (after [[AFC Ajax|Ajax]], [[AC Milan]], and [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]]) to win the UEFA Super Cup in consecutive seasons.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Stynes |first=Trevor |date=August 13, 2026 |title=PSG retain UEFA Super Cup with 2-1 win over Aston Villa |url=https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/psg-retain-uefa-super-cup-with-2-1-win-over-aston-villa-2026-08-12/ |archive-url= |access-date=August 28, 2026}}</ref>

